Moreover, statistical analyses were performed to compare the predictive performance across different input feature types. Initially, the Shapiro–Wilk test was used to evaluate the normality of the data. As the distributions were found to be non-normal, the non-parametric Kruskal–Wallis test was applied, followed by post-hoc pairwise comparisons using Dunn’s test. The results indicated that K-mer, One-hot encoding, and Seq_Tokens were the most effective input features for predicting antiviral activity of peptides. Notably, the differences among these three representations were not statistically significant (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$p \le 0.05$$\end{document}) across all evaluation metrics, except for a slight difference observed in the sensitivity metric. In contrast, AAC and Phychem features yielded lower performance metrics and showed statistically significant differences when compared with the aforementioned sequence-based representations. Figure 3 illustrates the statistical analysis outcomes, where features with and without significant differences are visually distinguished using different colors.

To gain deeper insights into the sequence patterns of the generated AVPs, motif discovery was performed using the MEME Suite tool. Table 3 presents three highly conserved motifs identified from the generated AVPs, each comprising 15 amino acid positions. Motif 1, represented by the sequence “RDIWDWICEVLSDFK,” shows the highest level of conservation across 58 sites, with an E-value of \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
